# shop.feeds — a small SHOP feeds table, the fixture for `nacs
# --assembly-report ... feeds=<file>` (DECISIONS.md → "the shop table reaches
# the report…").
#
# It exists to prove the precedence K3 was founded on: THE SHOP'S PROVEN
# NUMBERS OUTRANK THE SHIPPED ONES. Two records, chosen to exercise both sides
# of the resolution in one file:
#
#   * 6061 x Endmill SHADOWS the shipped record (150 m/min there, 260 here) —
#     a shop that has actually run this alloy on this machine and knows it
#     will take more. The report's rpm column moves because of it.
#   * 7075 x Endmill is a row the SHIPPED table does not hold at all, so it is
#     the other half: a shop can add a material we never vetted, which is the
#     whole reason a miss refuses by name instead of guessing.
#
# Nothing here shadows 6061 x Drill, so that lookup still falls through to the
# shipped table — the "shipped table behind it" the report header names.
#
# The numbers are this fixture's own and are documented as such in every
# source line, per the grammar's requirement that a record say where its
# numbers came from.

units mm

feeds 6061 Endmill
surface_speed 260
source fixture data for the shop-table precedence test — deliberately well above the shipped 150 m/min so the shadowing is visible in the rpm column
chipload 1..3 0.015
chipload 3..6 0.030
chipload 6..13 0.055

feeds 7075 Endmill
surface_speed 180
source fixture data — a material the shipped table does not hold at all, so this record is the only place the lookup can succeed
chipload 1..3 0.012
chipload 3..6 0.025
chipload 6..13 0.045
